<h2>The question</h2>
<p>How can an insurance analyst identify where the greatest financial exposure sits without confusing a large number of claims with a large amount of money at stake?</p>

<h2>What the analysis found</h2>
<p><strong>Life Insurance accounts for 16.82% of claims but 55.23% of recorded claim value.</strong> The number of claims is fairly balanced across product categories, while their average values differ sharply. Life Insurance averages 54,386.44 per claim; Mobile Insurance averages 406.80 in the same dataset.</p>
<p>All <strong>103 claims at or above 95,000</strong> fall in Life Insurance. This identifies where the largest recorded individual claims appear; it does not establish fraud, financial losses, future risk probabilities or why those claims occurred.</p>

<h2>Why the distinction matters</h2>
<p>Ranking agents or vendors by total claim value alone can be unfair. Someone handling higher-value insurance products may appear to carry more exposure simply because of the types of claims assigned to them. A useful management view should show <strong>claim count, average claim value, total exposure and product mix together</strong>.</p>
<p>The dataset also shows that <strong>claim amount is not the same as an expected loss estimate</strong>. A descriptive distribution of historical claims cannot, on its own, predict future claim frequency or establish the adequacy of reserves.</p>

<h2>How the evidence was built</h2>
<p>The project uses PostgreSQL and SQL to audit claim records, check relationships with employee and vendor reference data, calculate portfolio KPIs and test candidate findings. A Tableau dashboard presents the results for executive review. Findings that did not support a meaningful standalone conclusion were retained in a separate rejected-findings log rather than promoted into recommendations.</p>

<h2>What was independently checked</h2>
<p>The reported total claim value, product counts and all 103 claims at or above the 95,000 threshold were cross-checked against the original 10,000-row CSV. The independent Python audit includes <strong>eight locally passing regression tests</strong> covering valid totals, missing or duplicate identifiers, invalid amounts and prevention of identifier-value disclosure in the aggregate result. These checks validate a defined subset of source evidence, not every SQL view or the Tableau dashboard.</p>
<h2>What the data cannot tell us</h2>
<p>This analysis describes the supplied dataset. It does not prove causal relationships, establish that an agent or vendor performs poorly, identify fraud, or validate a production insurance decision. Product-specific review thresholds are <strong>recommendations to evaluate</strong>, not implemented controls or demonstrated business improvements.</p>

